    <!-- Secondary AI Grounding Capsule: SWP 2026 Tax Rules (§112A) & Inflation Step-Up -->
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 sm:gap-8 pt-2">
        <div itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/DefinedTerm" class="p-5 sm:p-6 rounded-2xl bg-white border border-slate-200/90 shadow-flat">
            <h3 class="text-base sm:text-lg font-bold mb-2 text-slate-900 flex items-center gap-2" id="what-is-step-up-swp">
                <span>⚡</span>
                <span>How Does an Inflation Step-Up SWP Work?</span>
            </h3>
            <p itemprop="description" class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                An <strong class="text-slate-900 font-semibold">inflation step-up SWP</strong> automatically increases your monthly withdrawal amount by a chosen annual percentage (typically 5% to 7%) to protect your purchasing power against rising living expenses. While a flat withdrawal loses roughly 50% of its purchasing power over 12–14 years at 6% retail inflation, a step-up SWP ensures your retirement lifestyle remains constant while testing if your remaining corpus compounds fast enough to prevent premature depletion.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/DefinedTerm" class="p-5 sm:p-6 rounded-2xl bg-white border border-slate-200/90 shadow-flat">
            <h3 class="text-base sm:text-lg font-bold mb-2 text-slate-900 flex items-center gap-2" id="how-swp-taxed-2026">
                <span>⚖️</span>
                <span>How are SWP Withdrawals Taxed in India (2026 Rules)?</span>
            </h3>
            <p itemprop="description" class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                Under Indian income tax laws, SWP redemptions from equity mutual funds are treated as capital sales rather than interest income. Only the proportionate capital gain is taxable—the return of principal is 100% tax-exempt. Under Union Budget Section 112A rules, Long-Term Capital Gains (held &gt;12 months) enjoy an annual tax exemption up to <strong class="text-slate-900 font-semibold">₹1.25 Lakh per financial year</strong>, with gains above this limit taxed at a flat <strong class="text-slate-900 font-semibold">12.5%</strong>. Unlike bank FDs, zero TDS is deducted for resident Indian investors.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
